  "schema_version": 1,
  "list_id": "claude-opus-5-5",
  "run": 2,
  "simulation_id": "sim-05",
  "member_id": "danielsson-ulf",
  "model": "gpt-5.6-terra",
  "reasoning_effort": "high",
  "prompt": "prompts/physics_committee_terra_round2_v1.md",
  "preferred_configuration": {
    "prize_parts": [
      {
        "ballot_id": "B014",
        "laureates": [
          "Hidetoshi Katori",
          "Jun Ye"
        ],
        "citation": "for the invention and development of optical lattice clocks"
      }
    ]
  },
  "alternatives": [
    "B032",
    "B035"
  ],
  "statement": {
    "case_for": "My configuration is unchanged: B014 remains the most complete discovery-led case. The magic-wavelength lattice-clock idea resolved the central conflict between trapping many atoms and preserving clock accuracy, and its realization has become an independently reproduced, systematically controlled instrument for precision measurement. Katori and Ye provide a compact attribution to the core conceptual and experimental achievement. Its importance rests on demonstrated physical capability in timekeeping, relativistic geodesy, and fundamental tests, not on any prospective redefinition of the second.",
    "case_against": "The principal reservation remains real. Optical-frequency metrology is a broad international enterprise, and a two-person award is defensible only if the citation is confined to the lattice-clock invention and defining development, rather than treated as recognition of optical clocks generally. The available discussion identifies this boundary but does not itself settle the historical priority question.",
    "responses": [
      {
        "member_id": "kroll-stefan",
        "point": "Kroll argues that the magic-wavelength principle, reproducible experimental realization, and demonstrated metrological reach make B014 a discrete and mature achievement, while requiring a narrow citation.",
        "response": "I agree. His distinction between the bounded lattice-clock advance and the wider optical-clock revolution is exactly what preserves both scientific precision and fair credit. The mature case is systematic control across laboratories, not a promise of future standards adoption."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "mehlig-bernhard",
        "point": "Mehlig favors B032 because Jarzynski, Crooks, and Seifert established a general framework for irreversibility, while acknowledging that it comprises related theorems and that its experimental prominence must be weighed against precision platforms.",
        "response": "B032 is a serious alternative and its conceptual breadth is attractive to a theorist. But the need to delimit several connected relations is not merely editorial: it makes the achievement less singular for a prize citation. B014 couples a sharply identifiable physical solution to direct, durable, and repeatedly testable experimental control; I therefore retain it above B032."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "lindroth-eva",
        "point": "Lindroth argues that the absence of an identified physical realization of inflation makes B035 less ready than the directly testable lattice-clock achievement.",
        "response": "I accept that this is the decisive present limitation on my original first-ranked alternative. Inflation and the quantum origin of structure remain profound and strongly motivated by precision cosmology, but the lack of a uniquely established microscopic mechanism weakens a bounded attribution to Guth, Linde, and Mukhanov. It remains an alternative, not my preferred award this year."
      }
    ],
    "uncertainties": [
      "A focused historical review is still needed to establish whether Katori and Ye alone adequately delimit the foundational optical-lattice-clock achievement and to exclude overlap with other essential optical-clock pioneers.",
      "The final citation should be tested for whether \"invention and development\" accurately captures the magic-wavelength concept and defining realization without claiming the entire optical-clock field."
    ],
    "conflict_note": "No conflict requiring recusal is apparent from the supplied profile."
  }
}
